# Lesson 21: Conjunctions and Correlative Pairs

## Definition
A Conjunction connects words, phrases, or sentences together.

---

## Types of Conjunctions

Before the correlative pairs below, it helps to know the three broad
families a conjunction can belong to:

### 1. Coordinating Conjunctions (FANBOYS)
Join two independent clauses of **equal** grammatical rank: `For`,
`And`, `Nor`, `But`, `Or`, `Yet`, `So`.
* *He was tired, **so** he went to bed early.*
* *She likes tea, **but** she prefers coffee.*

### 2. Subordinating Conjunctions
Join a dependent (subordinate) clause to an independent (main) clause:
`Because`, `Although`, `Since`, `While`, `If`, `Unless`, `Whereas`,
`As soon as`, `Provided that`, `In order that`.
* ***Although** he was tired, he finished the work.*
* *We will start **as soon as** she arrives.*

### 3. Correlative Conjunctions
Work in **pairs**, and each half of the pair must match a
grammatically parallel element on either side. This is the family the
rest of this lesson focuses on, since exam questions almost always
test whether the *correct partner* of the pair has been used.

---

## Mandatory Correlative Conjunction Pairs

| Conjunction 1 | Mandatory Pair | Meaning / Context |
| :--- | :--- | :--- |
| **Although / Though** | `Yet` or `,` (comma) | यद्यपि ... फिर भी |
| **Rather / Other / Otherwise** | `Than` | के बजाय / अन्यथा |
| **Nothing else** | `But` | और कुछ नहीं बल्कि |
| **Hardly / Scarcely** | `When` | मुश्किल से ... कि |
| **No sooner (did / had)** | `Than` | जैसे ही ... वैसे ही |
| **Not only** | `But also` | न केवल ... बल्कि |
| **Either** | `Or` | या तो ... या |
| **Neither** | `Nor` | न तो ... न ही |
| **Between** | `And` | के बीच |
| **Both** | `And` | दोनों ... और |
| **From** | `To` | से ... तक |
| **As ... As** | Positive Sentences | उतना ... जितना |
| **So ... As** | Negative Sentences | उतना ... जितना |
| **Whether** | `Or` | चाहे ... या |
| **As** | `So` | जैसे ... वैसे |
| **Such** | `That` | ऐसा ... कि |
| **So** | `That` | इतना ... कि |
| **Scarcely / Barely** | `When` / `Before` | मुश्किल से ... कि |
| **Provided / Providing** | `That` (often omitted) | बशर्ते कि |
| **In order** | `That` | ताकि |
| **In case** | (no pair — stands alone) | अगर ऐसा हुआ तो |

---

## Important Rules

### 1. Negative Words with Untill / Unless
`Until` (Time-bound) and `Unless` (Condition-bound) are negative words.
* **Do NOT use `not` or `never`** inside the `until/unless` clause.
* *Correct:* Wait here **until** I return.
* *Incorrect:* Wait here **until** I **don't** return.
* *Correct:* **Unless** you work hard, you will not succeed.
* *Incorrect:* **Unless** you **don't** work hard, you will not succeed.

### 2. Lest Rule
`Lest` takes **`Should`** (Never use *may*, *will*, or *not*).
* *Work hard **lest** you **should** fail.*
* *Incorrect:* Work hard **lest** you **may** fail.
* *He ran fast **lest** he **should** miss the train.*

### 3. Supposing / If Rule
`Suppose` and `If` mean the same thing; **never use them together**.
* *Correct:* **Suppose** it rains tomorrow, what will you do?
* *Correct:* **If** it rains tomorrow, what will you do?
* *Incorrect:* **If suppose** it rains tomorrow, what will you do?

### 4. Unreal Condition (As if)

@math
As if + Subject + were
@/math

* *He talks **as if** he **were** a minister.*
* *She behaves **as if** she **were** the boss.*
* *Note:* `were` is used for all subjects (I, he, she, it) in this
  unreal/hypothetical structure, not just plural ones.

### 5. Both ... And — Always Plural Verb
When two subjects are joined by `Both ... And`, the verb is always
**plural**, regardless of how many subjects it joins.
* *Correct:* **Both** Ram **and** Shyam **are** going to the market.
* *Incorrect:* **Both** Ram **and** Shyam **is** going to the market.

### 6. Either ... Or / Neither ... Nor — Verb Agrees with the Nearer Subject
When subjects joined by `Either ... Or` or `Neither ... Nor` differ in
number or person, the verb agrees with the subject **closer to it**.
* *Correct:* **Either** the manager **or** the employees **are**
  responsible.
* *Correct:* **Neither** the students **nor** the teacher **was**
  present.

### 7. Not Only ... But Also — Keep Structures Parallel
The grammatical structure following `Not only` must match the
structure following `But also`.
* *Correct:* She is **not only** intelligent **but also** hardworking.
* *Incorrect:* She is **not only** intelligent **but also** works hard.

### 8. As Well As — Verb Agrees with the First Subject
Unlike `Both ... And`, a phrase joined with `as well as` (or `along
with`, `together with`, `in addition to`) does **not** change the
number of the main verb — the verb still agrees with the **first**
subject only.
* *Correct:* The **manager**, as well as his employees, **is** happy.
* *Incorrect:* The manager, as well as his employees, **are** happy.

---

## Practice Exercises

Choose the correct conjunction / correction for each sentence.

1. *He behaves **as if** he ___ (was / were) the king of the house.*
   — **Answer:** were
2. ***Hardly** had I reached the station ___ (than / when) the train
   left.* — **Answer:** when
3. *Work sincerely **lest** you ___ (should fail / will fail).*
   — **Answer:** should fail
4. ***Not only** did he apologise ___ (but also / but) offered to pay
   for the damage.* — **Answer:** but also
5. ***Neither** the players **nor** the coach ___ (was / were) ready
   for the match.* — **Answer:** was (agrees with 'the coach')
6. *Wait here **until** I ___ (return / don't return).*
   — **Answer:** return (no negative word with until)
